INT. OPERA HOUSE - PROPERTY ROOM - 1912 - NIGHT

Costumes on hooks. Painted swords. Plaster saints. One lantern. Silence.

LADY CORDELIA ASHWORTH (30s, velvet cloak) kneels by a trunk. She lifts a lid. Reaches inside. Draws out a glass vial.

Behind her, MR. FERDINAND LACROIX (50s, theater manager, waistcoat) watches from a rack of wigs. He coughs. She does not turn.

                    FERDINAND
          Lady Ashworth. Still here?

                    CORDELIA
          Looking for a prop.

                    FERDINAND
          Ah.

He steps closer. She slips the vial into her sleeve. He smiles.

                    FERDINAND (CONT'D)
          Rehearsal ended hours ago.

                    CORDELIA
          I'm very dedicated.

A door bangs. Heavy boots. Voices from the corridor.

A broad figure fills the doorway: INSPECTOR GRIMSBY (40s, bowler, mud on his boots). He scans the room. His eyes settle on the trunk.

                    GRIMSBY
          Evening.

Ferdinand lunges for the lantern. Cordelia slams the trunk lid. Grimsby draws his truncheon. The lantern falls. Darkness.

Scuffle. A curse. Glass shatters. The lantern rolls and rights itself, still burning.

They stand frozen, three in a triangle. He holds the broken vial. She holds a fencing foil. He holds the truncheon.

                    GRIMSBY
          Well, then.
